WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:37.650
The whole fabric of America is bloody to its
very core.

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:42.380
And there’s no quicker way to find out someone
shares your bloodlust than when the subject

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:44.290
of serial killers comes up.

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:47.270
Think about the recent renaissance of true
crime shows.

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:50.270
Look at what some of the top rated podcasts
are.

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:54.720
People cannot get enough murder in their recreational
unwinding time.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:00.470
It’s a way of living vicariously, and maybe
it’s healthy overall, but it’s more than

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:02.000
a little dark.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.930
Serial killers and their tales of adventure
that captivated the public really seemed to

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:09.160
hit a stride in the 1960s and thereafter.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:14.030
The Zodiac Killer, Son of Sam, Ed Gein, and
John Wayne Gacy kept people glued to their

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:19.800
newspapers and news broadcasts to see what
awful, mesmerizing things they had done.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:25.220
The cities and towns where these killers operated
stayed gripped in fear during their unpredictable

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:30.440
and sporadic reigns of terror, and that was
just as true in the upstate New York town

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:32.990
of Rochester in the early 1970s.

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:38.850
There, between 1971 and 1973, someone was
kidnapping children and murdering them.

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:43.440
But that’s just the beginning of the weirdness
surrounding these cases.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.440
Here, we take a look at the Alphabet Murders.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:50.850
The First Murder

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:58.690
In the mid-afternoon of November 16, 1971,
10-year-old Carmen Colon was running errands

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:01.270
before returning back to her home in Rochester.

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:04.869
She made a stop at a pharmacy to pick up a
prescription for her grandmother, and while

00:02:04.869 --> 00:02:09.619
being informed that the medication was not
yet ready to be picked up, became noticeably

00:02:09.619 --> 00:02:11.120
distracted and upset.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:15.120
She told the owner of the pharmacy that she
“had to go”, and got into a car parked

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:16.230
nearby.

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:19.780
Three hours later, she was reported missing
to the Rochester Police Department.

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:23.670
In those few hours between her leaving the
pharmacy and becoming an official missing

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:28.580
person, she was observed on a local interstate,
half naked and flailing her arms for help.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:33.370
A vehicle identified as a dark Ford Pinto
was backing up frantically trying to run her

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:37.920
down, and eventually an individual overpowered
her and threw her back into the car.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:42.230
About thirty-eight motorists on the highway
witnessed the act, though not one soul attempted

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:43.970
to intervene in the apparent abduction.

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:49.200
None of the drivers who saw what happened
even thought to call it in until three days

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:50.200
later.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:52.700
Unfortunately, by that time, it was too late.

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:57.120
On November 18, two teenagers came across
the body of Carmen Colon, nearly twelve miles

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:59.840
from where she was seen struggling on the
interstate.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:02.920
Some of her clothing was in fact found near
that very highway.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:07.110
She had been raped, her skull had been fractured,
and she had been strangled to death.

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:11.310
Local newspapers soon offered up a meager
reward for whoever could offer information

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:15.090
leading to an arrest, and some billboards
were later erected asking for clues.

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:18.379
The potential suspects that would crop up
after the first murder and later would only

00:03:18.379 --> 00:03:22.150
add more perplexing questions to the cases.

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:27.230
The First Suspect

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:31.950
Miguel Colon was Carmen Colon’s uncle, and
after her parents split up he became even

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:33.629
closer to Carmen and her mother.

00:03:33.629 --> 00:03:37.040
When she would take her usual walks to the
pharmacy, she was usually accompanied by her

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:41.040
grandfather, Felix, but on the day of her
disappearance she somehow persuaded him to

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:42.430
let her travel alone.

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:45.459
This was the day that she got into a strange
car.

00:03:45.459 --> 00:03:49.930
Later on the highway, she was fleeing from
what was presumably the same car, a dark Ford

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:51.379
Pinto hatchback.

00:03:51.379 --> 00:03:54.819
Just before Carmen Colon went missing, her
uncle Miguel took possession of a car that

00:03:54.819 --> 00:03:57.550
eerily matched the car from the highway incident.

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:00.970
When authorities learned of this, they went
to Miguel’s home to search the vehicle.

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:05.190
During the investigation, they took note of
the car having recently been profusely cleaned,

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:09.130
especially the trunk, which had been gone
over in detail with a rather strong cleaning

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:10.130
product.

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:14.080
If you’ve paid attention to anyone’s car
trunk ever, you’ll know that this is a pretty

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:15.990
rare occurrence.

00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:19.150
Investigators asked the dealership if that
was a common practice, since it was a relatively

00:04:19.150 --> 00:04:21.150
new purchase on Miguel’s behalf.

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:23.940
The dealers stated that they had not conducted
such a cleaning.

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:28.620
More creepily, a doll of Carmen’s was found
in the car, which he explained off by saying

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:31.780
he was a family member, and that wasn’t
very strange at all.

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:35.440
What was strange, however, was Miguel’s
sudden panic two days after Carmen’s death,

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:39.380
in which he allegedly told a friend he had
to flee the country because he had “done

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:43.419
something wrong in Rochester”, and indeed
he did take off to Puerto Rico very soon after.

00:04:43.419 --> 00:04:46.440
You haven’t heard the last of Miguel Colon.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:53.070
The Second Murder

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:57.919
On an early April afternoon in 1972, more
than a year after Carmen Colon’s murder,

00:04:57.919 --> 00:05:01.810
11-year-old Wanda Walkowicz was heading to
her home after an errand, much like Carmen

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:02.810
had been.

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:04.740
In her hometown of Rochester, just like Carmen.

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:08.120
She was getting groceries, and headed back
down the road towards her house.

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:09.430
It was 5:15 pm.

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:12.630
Joyce, Wanda’s mother, reported her missing
around 8 pm.

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:16.480
A slew of detectives canvassed an enormous
area of land near her home.

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:20.930
They scoured the area around the grocery store
they knew she had been at before her disappearance,

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:24.770
and they searched near the local river where
they knew she would sometimes play.

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:29.560
Rescue teams heard a few straggling reports
of Wanda, grocery bags in hand, resting against

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:33.979
a fence to keep herself from dropping the
bags, as a dark brown vehicle approached her.

00:05:33.979 --> 00:05:37.470
10 am the next morning, a policeman found
her body at the base of a hill.

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:41.930
It had been apparently thrown from a moving
car, and rolled down to the ground below.

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:46.130
She had been sexually assaulted and choked
to death, much like Carmen the previous year.

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:49.220
She had fought her attacker, which was clear
from the defensive wounds.

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:53.370
Oddly, she had a noticeable amount of white
cat hair on her body, even though her family

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:54.660
owned no such animals.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:58.610
During the autopsy, custard was discovered
among her stomach contents, something her

00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:01.219
family swore she would not have eaten at home
or school.

00:06:01.219 --> 00:06:02.690
So was her killer feeding her before taking
on his nefarious task?

00:06:02.690 --> 00:06:07.270
Hotlines were set up, much larger rewards
of up to $10,000 were offered, and tips rolled

00:06:07.270 --> 00:06:08.349
in from the public.

00:06:08.349 --> 00:06:12.000
One tip came from someone who said they saw
Wanda speaking with the driver of that brown

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.970
car from the passenger side window just before
she went missing.

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:19.180
Another tip said they saw a man forcing a
girl matching Wanda’s looks into a light-colored

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:20.970
Dodge Dart-type automobile.

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:24.720
Fliers were plastered all over the surrounding
neighborhoods in the hopes that any shred

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:27.560
of evidence would point investigators in the
right direction.

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:31.940
The already-apparent surface similarities
between Carmen and Wanda’s cases were almost

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:34.530
immediately dismissed by the local police
department.

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:39.050
More suspects, more tips, and even more questions
ensued.

00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:44.170
The Third Victim

00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:49.790
Michelle Maenza was returning home from school
on November 26, 1973, just about seven months

00:06:49.790 --> 00:06:52.260
after the murder of Wanda Walkowicz.

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:55.490
The eleven-year-old stopped at a Rochester
department store on the way back, attempting

00:06:55.490 --> 00:06:57.640
to fetch a purse her mother had left there
earlier.

00:06:57.640 --> 00:07:01.690
Her uncle passed her at some point, offering
to give her a ride, but Michelle opted to

00:07:01.690 --> 00:07:02.880
go it alone.

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:06.979
Witnesses reported that soon after, she was
seen entering a tan or beige vehicle.

00:07:06.979 --> 00:07:08.620
This was not her uncle’s car.

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:10.870
Her mother reported her missing that evening.

00:07:10.870 --> 00:07:15.090
Around 4:30 pm, a girl who matched Maenza’s
features was seen eating at a fast food restaurant

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:21.010
with a man described as a Caucasian between
the ages of 25 and 35, and roughly 6 foot,

00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:22.040
165 lbs.

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:26.720
A person traveling down a nearby road around
5:30 pm that day said they saw the same beige

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:30.690
or tan color of vehicle as earlier in the
afternoon, but with a flat tire.

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:34.880
The motorist also apparently saw a girl that
looked very much like Michelle, and noticed

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:36.710
she was in a fair amount of distress.

00:07:36.710 --> 00:07:41.170
She was being held by the wrist by the driver
outside of the car, and when the approaching

00:07:41.170 --> 00:07:45.219
witness tried to stop and check on the girl,
he was met with a glare by the suspect.

00:07:45.219 --> 00:07:48.480
The kidnapper tried to hide the girl behind
him and also attempted to keep his license

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:49.640
plate out of sight.

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:53.090
He put off a menacing enough vibe that the
would-be helper drove off.

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:57.789
Two days later, on November 28th, Michelle
Maenza’s body was found in a ditch roughly

00:07:57.789 --> 00:07:59.200
15 miles from her home.

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:02.839
Just like the first two victims, she had been
sexually assaulted and then strangled with

00:08:02.839 --> 00:08:04.419
some kind of rope or cord.

00:08:04.419 --> 00:08:08.630
And just like Wanda Walkowicz, she had numerous
strands of white cat hair on her.

00:08:08.630 --> 00:08:12.860
Some small samples of prints and semen were
able to be recovered, and upon autopsy, her

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:17.260
stomach contents included traces of hamburger,
backing up the story of her being last seen

00:08:17.260 --> 00:08:18.370
at a fast food restaurant.

00:08:18.370 --> 00:08:22.820
A composite sketch of the man who was seen
with her was circulated but, like most of

00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:27.339
the tiny nuggets of possible leads that crept
up throughout the case, real concrete information

00:08:27.339 --> 00:08:32.599
was not to be had.

00:08:32.599 --> 00:08:34.709
Eerie Similarities

00:08:34.709 --> 00:08:37.060
So why were these dubbed the “Alphabet Murders”?

00:08:37.060 --> 00:08:41.190
Well, you may have noticed that all three
victims had first and last names that started

00:08:41.190 --> 00:08:42.500
with the same letter.

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:46.810
As time has gone on, experts think that the
likelihood that this was intentional is very

00:08:46.810 --> 00:08:47.810
low.

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:51.560
But even if the killer had chosen his victims
on this basis, that’s not even the strangest

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:53.640
of the similarities in the crimes.

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:56.080
There’s the final resting place of each
body.

00:08:56.080 --> 00:08:59.750
All three children were from Rochester, but
when their bodies were discovered, it was

00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:03.290
in a different town, and each town corresponded
to their names.

00:09:03.290 --> 00:09:07.769
Carmen Colon’s body was found in the town
of Churchville, Wanda Walkowicz was 7 miles

00:09:07.769 --> 00:09:12.380
away from Rochester in the village of Webster,
and Michelle Maenza was found in Macedon.

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:15.620
While you could argue that the names were
coincidences, it’s hard to argue that the

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:19.920
killer didn’t somehow plan to dump each
victim in a place that matched their name

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:20.920
lettering.

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:23.839
There’s the obvious fact that each of the
victims was a pre-teen girl.

00:09:23.839 --> 00:09:27.470
They all three disappeared during rainy days,
were of a short height, and were all seen

00:09:27.470 --> 00:09:29.779
as loner types, without many close friends.

00:09:29.779 --> 00:09:33.259
The positioning of each body when they were
found seemed to point to them all being thrown

00:09:33.259 --> 00:09:34.290
from a vehicle.

00:09:34.290 --> 00:09:38.589
Each girl was brought up in a poor Catholic
family and had histories with being bullied

00:09:38.589 --> 00:09:42.820
and having issues with academic performance
in school, which gave investigators some inkling

00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:48.050
that the murderer may have had a background
in social services, and thus had access to

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:51.610
knowing these very specific details about
the girls.

00:09:51.610 --> 00:09:57.089
More Suspects, Fewer Answers

00:09:57.089 --> 00:10:01.640
Miguel Colon, as we mentioned, spirited away
to Puerto Rico four short days after his niece

00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:02.640
was killed.

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:07.149
In March of 1972, investigators made their
way down there to begin asking him questions

00:10:07.149 --> 00:10:08.850
about her disappearance and murder.

00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:12.910
The fact that he had a car that resembled
the one seen during the time of Carmen’s

00:10:12.910 --> 00:10:17.180
kidnapping, and the suspicious extent to which
the vehicle had been cleaned gave them plenty

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:18.279
of reasonable doubt.

00:10:18.279 --> 00:10:22.490
However, down in Puerto Rico, newspapers caught
wind of the authorities’ desire to interview

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:26.120
him and published articles that Miguel saw,
causing him to flee.

00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:29.860
He did return later in March, and was extradited
back to New York state.

00:10:29.860 --> 00:10:33.529
During questioning, Miguel was not able to
provide a sufficient excuse for where he was

00:10:33.529 --> 00:10:38.220
during Carmen’s disappearance, and had no
other people that could back him up regarding

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:39.730
his location that day.

00:10:39.730 --> 00:10:44.089
Despite strong suspicion that they had their
guy, in the end there was no physical evidence

00:10:44.089 --> 00:10:47.819
that could definitively link him or his car
to the crimes.

00:10:47.819 --> 00:10:51.620
In 1991, twenty years after his niece Carmen
was killed, Miguel took his own life after

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:55.800
being involved in a dispute at home where
he shot his brother and his wife.

00:10:55.800 --> 00:11:00.089
The good samaritan who tried to approach the
individual who had captured Michelle Maenza

00:11:00.089 --> 00:11:01.190
was a bit of help.

00:11:01.190 --> 00:11:04.829
Even though the kidnapper tried to block him
from seeing his license plate, that person

00:11:04.829 --> 00:11:07.829
was able to read off a partial plate to the
authorities.

00:11:07.829 --> 00:11:11.620
As luck would have it, he ran across the same
car several days later, and was able to provide

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:13.189
the full plate number.

00:11:13.189 --> 00:11:17.339
This led police to a man living with his family
in Lyons, New York, just over 40 miles from

00:11:17.339 --> 00:11:18.339
Rochester.

00:11:18.339 --> 00:11:22.449
This new suspect had a beige-colored sedan,
a strong resemblance to witness sketches,

00:11:22.449 --> 00:11:25.639
and had been in trouble with the law for petty
crimes for quite a while.

00:11:25.639 --> 00:11:29.300
His alibi to the police was that he had been
out looking for work that entire day.

00:11:29.300 --> 00:11:33.639
Upon further investigation and perusing of
phone records, it seemed that his alibi was

00:11:33.639 --> 00:11:34.639
airtight.

00:11:34.639 --> 00:11:36.029
He even passed a polygraph.

00:11:36.029 --> 00:11:39.730
The suspect never had his name released, and
even though he might have been the closest

00:11:39.730 --> 00:11:42.880
the police got to a real lead, it never panned
out.

00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:45.970
Dennis Termini was another potential lead
in the Alphabet Murders.

00:11:45.970 --> 00:11:50.730
A local firefighter in the Rochester area,
Termini was also known locally as the “Garage

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:56.209
Rapist”, and had raped at least fourteen
females between the years of 1971 and 1973,

00:11:56.209 --> 00:11:58.509
which are the years that the Alphabet Murders
occurred.

00:11:58.509 --> 00:12:02.750
Termini owned a beige-colored car, which was
the color vehicle that had been seen at more

00:12:02.750 --> 00:12:04.120
than one of the abductions.

00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:08.319
He also resided on a street very close to
the area where witnesses last reported seeing

00:12:08.319 --> 00:12:09.319
Michelle Maenza.

00:12:09.319 --> 00:12:12.821
Michelle Maenza was thought to be the last
victim of the “Alphabet Murders”, but

00:12:12.821 --> 00:12:17.029
five weeks after her death, Dennis Termini
tried to pull a gun on a teenage girl with

00:12:17.029 --> 00:12:18.529
the intent of abducting her.

00:12:18.529 --> 00:12:22.240
She started screaming, which led him to flee
the scene, but he obviously didn’t learn

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:25.290
his lesson and tried again soon after to kidnap
another girl.

00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:27.519
Police were made aware, and started after
him.

00:12:27.519 --> 00:12:31.500
Termini was not going to be captured alive,
and so he shot himself in the head.

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:35.230
Years later, forensic studies were conducted
with Termini’s exhumed corpse.

00:12:35.230 --> 00:12:39.279
They compared DNA from his body to semen samples
that were saved from Wanda Walkowicz, the

00:12:39.279 --> 00:12:40.279
second Alphabet victim.

00:12:40.279 --> 00:12:41.279
Nothing matched.

00:12:41.279 --> 00:12:45.209
Unfortunately, no evidence was available from
Michelle Maenza’s body, the one victim they

00:12:45.209 --> 00:12:47.339
had him most closely tied to.

00:12:47.339 --> 00:12:51.879
His car, however, had all sorts of white car
hair on the surfaces within.

00:12:51.879 --> 00:12:54.870
White cat hair was found on two of the Alphabet
victims.

00:12:54.870 --> 00:12:59.180
Kenneth Bianchi drew police interest because
he lived and worked near the first two murder

00:12:59.180 --> 00:13:00.180
locations.

00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:03.770
He held jobs as an ice cream man, a security
guard, and an ambulance driver, which made

00:13:03.770 --> 00:13:07.580
investigators think he wore many uniforms
that would make children approach him and

00:13:07.580 --> 00:13:08.580
trust him.

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:12.649
He too had a car that matched the description
passed around as being near the scenes of

00:13:12.649 --> 00:13:13.649
the Alphabet abductions.

00:13:13.649 --> 00:13:18.100
Biancha moved to California in 1976, three
years after the last killing.

00:13:18.100 --> 00:13:21.480
He, along with his cousin, became a serial
killer on the west coast, becoming the Hillside

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:22.480
Strangler.

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:28.430
Between 1977 and 1978, they murdered ten females
between the ages of 12 and 28, though Biancha

00:13:28.430 --> 00:13:31.560
strongly denies having anything to do with
the Alphabet murders.

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:36.310
Yet another suspect was Joseph Naso, who did
his own series of killings in California between

00:13:36.310 --> 00:13:38.310
1977 and 1994.

00:13:38.310 --> 00:13:42.839
His victims were mostly believed to be prostitutes,
but one interesting detail that came up was

00:13:42.839 --> 00:13:47.240
that all the females he killed had first and
last names that began with the same letter,

00:13:47.240 --> 00:13:48.660
just like the Alphabet murders.

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:52.570
Naso was also a Rochester, New York native,
and lived there in the early 1970s.

00:13:52.570 --> 00:13:57.390
In fact, he often traveled back and forth
between New York and California, and this

00:13:57.390 --> 00:13:59.269
wasn’t lost on investigators.

00:13:59.269 --> 00:14:03.120
The same samples that were tested from Wanda
Walkowicz’s body against Dennis Termini

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:06.600
were tested against Naso’s DNA, and did
not match.

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:10.810
Naso was convicted of his own California Alphabet
murders, however, and was sentenced to death

00:14:10.810 --> 00:14:12.560
in 2013.

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:17.569
When police searched his house more thoroughly,
they found an absolutely insane journal where

00:14:17.569 --> 00:14:22.260
Naso kept the details of his murders.

00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:25.529
An Eternal Mystery

00:14:25.529 --> 00:14:29.310
Obviously, the Alphabet Murders remain unsolved
to this day.

00:14:29.310 --> 00:14:32.910
Little pieces of evidence have pointed to
many potential suspects, but none of it has

00:14:32.910 --> 00:14:35.370
been indisputable enough to land a conviction.

00:14:35.370 --> 00:14:39.290
It’s not even truly known if all three murders
are definitely connected.

00:14:39.290 --> 00:14:43.740
Tips rolled in from the public, and new suspects
had a brief light shining on them, but then

00:14:43.740 --> 00:14:44.910
something wouldn’t connect.

00:14:44.910 --> 00:14:48.389
A polygraph would pass, or an alibi would
line up.

00:14:48.389 --> 00:14:53.449
Did Joseph Naso merely move to California
and continue killing there, retaining the

00:14:53.449 --> 00:14:54.449
alphabet method?

00:14:54.449 --> 00:14:58.029
Was it someone else entirely, and they just
moved somewhere else and killed there?

00:14:58.029 --> 00:15:02.399
Did they feel like the law was closing in
after the third murder, and they somehow quelled

00:15:02.399 --> 00:15:03.620
the urge to kill again?

00:15:03.620 --> 00:15:08.270
There are so many questions and zero sense
of closure from the families.

00:15:08.270 --> 00:15:11.319
Even the experts have a hard time drawing
conclusions.

00:15:11.319 --> 00:15:14.930
One such expert was Robert Ressler, who actually
came up with the term “serial killer”

00:15:14.930 --> 00:15:19.389
and who spent decades profiling them and trying
to get inside their heads.

00:15:19.389 --> 00:15:23.529
Ressler worked at the FBI, in the Behavioral
Studies Unit, where he got to examine the

00:15:23.529 --> 00:15:27.279
actions and thoughts of some of the most horrid
people that ever lived.

00:15:27.279 --> 00:15:30.990
Ressler actually did not believe the same
person conducted all three killings.

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:34.569
When he drew up profiles of the killers, he
believed that Carmen’s killing showed a

00:15:34.569 --> 00:15:36.870
much higher level of anger and aggression.

00:15:36.870 --> 00:15:40.649
She also had not received food before her
death, like the other two girls seemingly

00:15:40.649 --> 00:15:41.649
did.

00:15:41.649 --> 00:15:45.199
Finally, the killer choked her with his hands,
whereas Wanda and Michelle had ligatures used

00:15:45.199 --> 00:15:46.199
on them.

00:15:46.199 --> 00:15:50.220
The experts also seemed to believe that the
matching first and last initials of each victim

00:15:50.220 --> 00:15:51.309
was purely coincidence.

00:15:51.309 --> 00:15:54.959
They believed that level of thought required
organizational skill that the killer simply

00:15:54.959 --> 00:15:56.120
did not have.

00:15:56.120 --> 00:15:59.870
The murders, the brutal dumping of the bodies,
and even the kidnappings themselves had no

00:15:59.870 --> 00:16:01.839
methodical orchestration to them, Ressler’s
team concluded.

00:16:01.839 --> 00:16:05.379
Is it possible that the killer’s trial run
with Carmen didn’t go according to plan,

00:16:05.379 --> 00:16:08.759
and that he got better at his work with the
next two?

00:16:08.759 --> 00:16:14.759
Of course, but we can’t say.

00:16:14.759 --> 00:16:16.759
Three Epilogues

00:16:16.759 --> 00:16:21.100
The New York State Police, City of Rochester
police, and two neighboring county departments

00:16:21.100 --> 00:16:24.160
all still have open cases for the girls who
were killed.

00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:27.780
The departments still work closely together
and meet to discuss new details when they

00:16:27.780 --> 00:16:28.780
come up.

00:16:28.780 --> 00:16:31.029
New technology being much more advanced than
what was at the police’s disposal in the

00:16:31.029 --> 00:16:36.329
1970s, they are now able to test genetics
and swab for DNA to create new suspects or

00:16:36.329 --> 00:16:37.660
eliminate past ones.

00:16:37.660 --> 00:16:41.449
Databases are available to instantly access
information from decades ago.

00:16:41.449 --> 00:16:45.319
The original detectives meet with the new
guard to pick each others’ brains and share

00:16:45.319 --> 00:16:46.319
leads.

00:16:46.319 --> 00:16:49.680
They all share the same sense of wanting to
honor the memories of the girls and help the

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:54.220
families close a door that’s been maddeningly
left open for so long.

00:16:54.220 --> 00:17:10.079
Carmen Colon, Wanda Walkowicz, and Michelle
Maena all share the same cemetery in Rochester

00:17:10.079 --> 00:17:11.620
as their final resting place.

